#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166572 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32afd19-5779-56f0-ad6d-1d6b541899f8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Even Extension of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "b0dbd2f9-8fef-5af8-b4ce-a371e65c6441" :question/math-academy-id "e-21830" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=x^3 - x + 5\\cos x$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x \\in (-\\pi,\\pi).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in(-\\pi,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in[0,\\pi),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n![](images/76/766b5034dc8f2dd6f3eb8e87f22f5156f58e9ab3aaebd90fa8770d6912d46e67.png)\n\nIn our cases, for $x\\in(-\\pi,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{3}-(-x)+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166573 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166959 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1e90af1-3fbd-5aa3-ac32-ac54c27c7224" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "43229a59-0b01-55ff-8b10-b1238360d277" :question/math-academy-id "e-21831" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=-6x-2$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi).$ Find the missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x).$ \n\n![](images/ee/ee53aca390d2803fa92f3239dc7548792c6be9e4c05a102fe936aeb9e7ced6d6.png)" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\na_n=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-6x-2$ for $x \\in (0,\\pi).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi (-6x-2) \\cos(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-6x-2\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-6 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[(-6x-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{6}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((-6\\pi -2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{6}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(0-\\frac{6}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{12(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi ))}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\cos(n\\pi)=(-1)^n,$ we have\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{12(1-(-1)^n)}{\\pi n^2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim (-2-3\\pi) + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{12(1-(-1)^n)}{\\pi n^2}} \n\\cdot \n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\cos(nx)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166574 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166958 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96ac89a1-4179-5afe-8c1b-ebd9d47bde79" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Constant Term From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "c8b4459b-e0ed-56cc-ab06-5be09baa7363" :question/math-academy-id "e-21833" :question/problem "$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3x}{4}\\right)$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for $a_0.$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\na_0=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3x}{4}\\right)$ for $x \\in (0,\\pi).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4})]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3\\pi }(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\pi }(\\sqrt{2}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32afd19-5779-56f0-ad6d-1d6b541899f8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Even Extension of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "593a5576-438c-5a88-b1ab-4d454dbfe49e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312688} {:answer/id #uuid "8b20e797-c5ce-5f0c-aa2b-9769c0a817b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313082} {:answer/id #uuid "914eefbb-7b51-5a10-b982-aac4cf2c32e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|-{x}^{2}+2x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313133} {:answer/id #uuid "ce996b3c-559e-5ad2-a307-7344e79bfbdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313589} {:answer/id #uuid "db8d13f9-28a0-54cd-893d-2c8b3f144b85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "|x{|}^{2}+2|x|" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b20e797-c5ce-5f0c-aa2b-9769c0a817b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313082} :answer-field/id #uuid "597899e8-1ef3-5f83-bfcf-33d3f5ac1fde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fcf50127-6a15-5bfa-b137-f8e9738bae25" :question/math-academy-id "q-340794" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{2}-2(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166959 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1e90af1-3fbd-5aa3-ac32-ac54c27c7224" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a426efb7-e051-5a26-9e22-009309b04e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (πx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164949} {:answer/id #uuid "d31d0e48-5be6-579e-b207-aca03d5cbfc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164950} {:answer/id #uuid "f26824fa-4fcc-58aa-8d29-69ee0c6b53a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos (nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164951} {:answer/id #uuid "7730fbef-6b3e-5bfb-8cae-351002b5c01a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos (πx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164952}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6eb4b0f3-7bad-579d-a2a9-a9b4d07fac23" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164948} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea687929-b165-5750-99db-0677d09cb370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4(-1)^{n})/(πn^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164954} {:answer/id #uuid "1d5b3ab1-e58c-501e-b9fb-9f664e466de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2(1 - (-1)^{n}))/(πn)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164955} {:answer/id #uuid "3c6c464e-1f5f-5ed6-b73a-de6ce3b08a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4((-1)^{n} - 1))/(πn^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164956} {:answer/id #uuid "3ea4d40b-5d48-584b-9bab-56ec1f3b8987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2(-1)^{n})/(πn)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164957}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8bc993b7-9a19-50f4-ac56-028c499dcaa2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164953}] :question/id #uuid "a2a82489-ea8c-529e-9b98-bcb721239fc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-340812" :question/problem "Let $F(x) = 2x + 3$ for $x ∈ [0, π)$. Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2π$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.$(4(-1)^{n})/(πn^{2})$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166959 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1e90af1-3fbd-5aa3-ac32-ac54c27c7224" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d405be31-c897-5a5f-b03c-7479a24a8ca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372780} {:answer/id #uuid "15dadc35-138b-57ce-b3ac-b3fe9918ee71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372781} {:answer/id #uuid "df26ab1b-10f0-5fb7-920d-db263c7db5d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(1+(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372782} {:answer/id #uuid "8fcd8ece-f8ce-5d23-b662-44e15bf7527c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372783} {:answer/id #uuid "b0465015-fc58-5606-97b8-d8faff54c375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fcd8ece-f8ce-5d23-b662-44e15bf7527c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372783} :answer-field/id #uuid "a83db56c-7d88-58da-8319-938cb2e5a2c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11bb39b9-16dc-501b-bed4-b3e8e0a1cfa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-341264" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-3x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{n}.$$\\,{a}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}\nfor $n\\ge 1$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-3x$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-\\frac{6}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{n\\pi }(0+\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{6(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi ))}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{6(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372785 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166958 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96ac89a1-4179-5afe-8c1b-ebd9d47bde79" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Constant Term From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83820c0d-0a83-57e5-b5c4-09ed20e945d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi (4\\pi -1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83820c0d-0a83-57e5-b5c4-09ed20e945d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi (4\\pi -1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372787} :answer-field/id #uuid "740f5300-6e19-5763-8bff-936052cc0268" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb464bbb-dddf-54fd-a602-93860ff93834" :question/math-academy-id "q-341278" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=12{x}^{2}-\\pi $ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=12{x}^{2}-\\pi $ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(12{x}^{2}-\\pi )\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[12\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\pi x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(4{\\pi }^{3}-{\\pi }^{2})-0 \\\\  & =2\\pi (4\\pi -1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166958 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96ac89a1-4179-5afe-8c1b-ebd9d47bde79" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Constant Term From the Half-Range Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70a30cec-1c74-58b6-a352-b55dbaad5ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(\\sqrt{3}-2)}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70a30cec-1c74-58b6-a352-b55dbaad5ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6(\\sqrt{3}-2)}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312860} :answer-field/id #uuid "850f065e-3ad8-58f4-8d41-45b829bfc32b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a1a98a94-1685-5290-8055-415073e9036d" :question/math-academy-id "q-341285" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{6(\\sqrt{3}-2)}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
